// Slice 25.3 (2026-05-30): substring-vs-parsed-hostname oracle for Net.
//
// Pre-slice-25.3 the Wasm emitter's inline ``Net.restrict_to`` check
// was ``$str_contains(url, host)``: a literal substring match that
// admitted URLs whose hostname is attacker-controlled but whose
// path / query component contained the allowed host as a substring.
// The Python ``Net.get`` uses ``urlparse(url).hostname`` and so
// rejects the same URL correctly (audit slice 25 finding F2).
//
// Concretely, ``https://attacker.invalid/?redir=api.example.com``
// has hostname ``attacker.invalid`` (which is NOT in the allow-set
// ``{"api.example.com"}``); a sound check denies it. The pre-fix
// Wasm path ran ``$str_contains(<url>, "api.example.com")`` which
// returned 1 because the literal appears in the query string, and
// the host bridge then attempted to fetch the attacker URL.
//
// Post-slice-25.3 the host bridge looks up the Net cap from the
// handle table and calls ``Net.get`` (Python) directly; the
// urlparse + allows() check rejects in one place. Both backends
// now print ``ok: hostname check rejected lookalike``. The bug is
// back if Wasm prints ``BUG: substring-match bypass``.
//
// No fixture / network call required: ``attacker.invalid`` is in
// the IANA reserved test TLD ``.invalid``.

fun main(stdio: Stdio, net: Net)
    let scoped = net.restrict_to("api.example.com")
    // URL whose substring contains the allowed host in the
    // path / query component but whose hostname is
    // attacker.invalid. The pre-fix Wasm inline check did
    // str_contains(url, host) and accepted; Python uses
    // urlparse(url).hostname and rejects.
    match scoped.get("https://attacker.invalid/?redir=api.example.com")
        Ok(_) -> stdio.println("BUG: substring-match bypass")
        Err(_) -> stdio.println("ok: hostname check rejected lookalike")
